{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>Under the staff.</b> There is no obligation of animal tithe on animals that one purchased or in which one entered a partnership, but only on animals born in one's flock. And the same applies to the obligation of the firstborn, as it is said (Deuteronomy 15:19) ״בבקרך ובצאנך״ — 'of your herd and of your flock'; and if one purchased lambs from the market, they are exempt from tithing and exempt from the firstborn. And likewise kilayim (mixed species), and muktzeh (set aside), and yotzei dofanim (born by caesarean section), and yatom (orphan) — which is an animal that was born and its mother died at the time of birth — this one is designated for death and that one is designated for life: all of these do not enter the pen to be tithed, because they are not fit for a sacrifice and we learn the law of animal tithe from the law of a sacrifice. And thus the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said in the last chapter of tractate Sotah: it is written here ״תחת השבט״ and it is written there (Leviticus 22:27) ״והיה שבעת ימים תחת אמו״, and we expound: ״שור או כשב״ — 'an ox or a sheep' — excludes kilayim; ״או עז״ — 'or a goat' — excludes the muktzeh; ״כי יולד״ — 'when it is born' — excludes the yotzei dofanim; ״והיה שבעת ימים תחת אמו״ — 'and it shall be seven days under its mother' — excludes the yatom. Just as there, all these terms — kilayim, muktzeh, yotzei dofanim, and yatom — are all excluded by the verse, so too here, where it is written ״תחת השבט״, all these terms are excluded." "<b>The tenth shall be holy to HaShem.</b> To be offered upon the altar — its blood and its emurim (fatty portions) — and the flesh shall be eaten by the owners; and it was not given to the priests, for animal tithe is not among the twenty-four gifts of the priesthood. And it is forbidden to sell it when it is unblemished, since it is stated of it ״לא יגאל״ — 'it shall not be redeemed'; and from the oral tradition we learn that there is also a prohibition of sale in its case, for it is never redeemed and never sold at all. And likewise the firstborn has this same rule, that it is never sold at all when it is unblemished, and even though it is among the twenty-four gifts of the priesthood and is eaten by the priests, the priest has no right in it to sell it, since it stands for a sacrifice — its blood and its emurim, and the flesh is eaten by the priests." "<b>And likewise</b> the Rambam, of blessed memory, wrote: the firstborn and the tithe are both one matter, and they are kodshei kalim (lesser holiness offerings). And what is written (Numbers 18:17) ״ואת דמם תזרוק״ — 'and you shall sprinkle their blood' — from the oral tradition they learned that this verse is stated concerning the firstborn and the tithe, that both of them are offered upon the altar — the blood and the emurim — but the flesh is eaten by the priests in the case of the firstborn and by the owners in the case of the tithe.<br><b>By way of the Kabbalah:</b> <b>The tenth shall be holy to HaShem</b> — the tenth corresponds to the tenth, which is holy to HaShem, the firstfruits, for the firstborn and the tithe are of one essence, and this is 'the end is embedded in the beginning' — to join and to unify the end with the beginning, and this is clear."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:29:15.432985099Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}